<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  husky_mcity_gazebo.launch - the Husky in Mcity, the OSRF recreation of
  Michigan's autonomous-vehicle test facility.

  A thin wrapper around husky_real_gazebo.launch, changing only what this
  world needs. Nothing else is duplicated: two launch files describing the
  same robot would drift apart the first time either was edited.

  THE MODEL PATH IS THE ONE THING THAT MUST BE RIGHT
    The mcity mesh lives in this package under models/, not in Gazebo's
    default search path, so GAZEBO_MODEL_PATH is extended below. Without it
    the world loads with the sun and physics but NO GEOMETRY, and the robot
    falls forever through an empty scene. $(optenv ...) preserves whatever
    was already on the path rather than replacing it.

  THE TOWN WAS RESCALED ON 2026-08-21 (0.254 -> 0.127)
    The mesh scale was wrong by a factor of two. The full argument is in
    models/mcity/model.sdf; the short version is that it made every kerb
    0.316 m == 1.9x the Husky's wheel radius, an impassable wall. Halving
    it gives a 0.158 m kerb (the US 6-inch standard, just under one wheel
    radius) and shrinks the town from 584x925 m to 292x463 m.

    So the spawn pose below is the OLD one halved, and it lands on the same
    piece of sidewalk as before == the town did not move, it shrank about
    its own origin.

  WHY THE SPAWN POSE IS WHAT IT IS
    Mcity is 292 m x 463 m. Where you start matters enormously, and not for
    the obvious reason.

    The first choice here was the sidewalk patch nearest the mesh origin.
    It was measured and it FAILS: 6 ORB keypoints on the decimated image
    against the 20 RTAB-Map needs. That spot is a wide concrete apron
    facing open ground, and Mcity's Concrete_Ground texture is near-uniform
    pale grey. Tiling textures are necessary for features but not
    sufficient == they also have to have CONTRAST, and a big flat pale
    surface under empty sky has none.

    Relative feature richness across the town, from the pre-rescale survey.
    The COUNTS BELOW ARE STALE == they were measured at 0.254, and halving
    the world changes how much of each surface fills the frame. Their
    RANKING is still the useful part; re-measure the count before any
    recorded run.

        -12.375,  -56.140   was 812   beside building cluster   <== default
        -34.325,  -39.335   was 154   building cluster C
        -19.670,  -54.125   was 137   dense buildings
        -57.230,  -13.880   was  39   west buildings, marginal
         -4.000,  -14.275   was   6   open concrete apron, FAILS
         -2.070,  -54.125   was   0   camera ends up inside geometry

    Sidewalk surfaces now sit at z = 0.158 (they were 0.316 before the
    rescale) == Mcity's sidewalks are genuinely raised above the roadway ==
    so spawn_z 0.45 drops the robot cleanly onto one. It should settle at
    0.158 + 0.13228 = 0.290.

    IF YOU MOVE THE SPAWN, RE-MEASURE. Feature richness varies by more than
    two orders of magnitude across this map.

  THE SPAWN HEADING MAKES THE SCENE
    yaw 2.85 points the robot down the sidewalk along -x. That puts the
    street on its LEFT, with the parked cars added to mcity.world beside
    it, and the pedestrians ahead on the walking line. Turning the robot
    around does not break anything, but it does throw away the scene.

  WHAT THIS WORLD OFFERS THAT THE OTHERS DO NOT
    Real kerb reveals, crosswalks, lane markings, buildings, and a road
    network with intersections and a roundabout. It is also the only world
    here with genuine height changes on the pedestrian route, which makes
    it the one candidate for the Reg/Force3DoF question besides
    sidewalk.world's synthetic camber.

  USAGE
    roslaunch sidewalk_sim husky_mcity_gazebo.launch gui:=true rviz:=false zedx:=true

  BEFORE ANY RECORDED RUN, measure the feature count (see ENGINEERING_NOTES.md). This
  mesh passes on paper == its UVs tile rather than stretch == but paper is
  not the running system, and the rescale has not yet been measured live.
-->
<launch>

  <arg name="gui"   default="true"/>
  <arg name="rviz"  default="true"/>
  <arg name="zedx"  default="false"/>
  <arg name="zedx_parent" default="zedx_base_frame"/>
  <arg name="zedx_xyz"    default="0 0 0"/>
  <arg name="zedx_rpy"    default="0 0 0"/>
  <arg name="zedx_stereo" default="false"/>
  <arg name="zedx_noise_stddev" default="0.005"/>

  <arg name="spawn_x"   default="-12.375"/>
  <arg name="spawn_y"   default="-56.140"/>
  <arg name="spawn_z"   default="0.45"/>
  <arg name="spawn_yaw" default="2.85"
       doc="Points down the sidewalk along -x, street on the left. See the
            header: this heading is what puts the cars and pedestrians in
            frame."/>

  <!-- Extend, do not replace, the model search path. -->
  <env name="GAZEBO_MODEL_PATH"
       value="$(find sidewalk_sim)/models:$(optenv GAZEBO_MODEL_PATH)"/>

  <include file="$(find sidewalk_sim)/launch/husky_real_gazebo.launch">
    <arg name="world"     value="$(find sidewalk_sim)/worlds/mcity.world"/>
    <arg name="spawn_x"   value="$(arg spawn_x)"/>
    <arg name="spawn_y"   value="$(arg spawn_y)"/>
    <arg name="spawn_z"   value="$(arg spawn_z)"/>
    <arg name="spawn_yaw" value="$(arg spawn_yaw)"/>

    <arg name="gui"         value="$(arg gui)"/>
    <arg name="rviz"        value="$(arg rviz)"/>
    <arg name="zedx"        value="$(arg zedx)"/>
    <arg name="zedx_parent" value="$(arg zedx_parent)"/>
    <arg name="zedx_xyz"    value="$(arg zedx_xyz)"/>
    <arg name="zedx_rpy"    value="$(arg zedx_rpy)"/>
    <arg name="zedx_stereo" value="$(arg zedx_stereo)"/>
    <arg name="zedx_noise_stddev" value="$(arg zedx_noise_stddev)"/>
  </include>

</launch>
